<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1455">1455</a>–<a href="/years/1495">1495</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as João II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II of Portugal was a king of the late fifteenth century who vigorously promoted exploration along the African coast. During his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> rounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, and he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His policies were central to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John II of Portugal" width="309" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IOANNES_QVARTVS_PORTVGALIAE_REX_(Kunsthistorisches_Museum).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John, called the Perfect Prince, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 3 May 1455, the second son of Afonso V of Portugal and Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. At one month old he was declared legitimate heir and received an oath of allegiance from the three estates. In 1471 he married his first cousin Eleanor of Viseu, and he was knighted that year after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asilah">Arzila</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. At Toro in March 1476 he defeated the Castilian right wing. From 1477 to 1481 he and his father were practically co-rulers, and he helped negotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> of 1479 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which gave Portugal hegemony in the Atlantic south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John became king on his father's death on 28 August 1481. He set out to reduce the power the nobility had gained, requiring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a> to swear allegiance to him as their superior and taking from nobles the right to administer justice on their estates. Fernando, Duke of Braganza, was arrested, convicted of treason and beheaded in June 1483. In September 1484 John himself stabbed to death Diogo, Duke of Viseu, his cousin and the brother of the queen, after confronting him with evidence of treason.</p><p>African trade became a crown monopoly, and its profits paid for exploration. The fortress of São Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> was founded in 1482, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a> found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> in 1484, and in 1488 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> rounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. John received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> twice, in 1484 and 1488, and rejected his plan, as the king's advisers judged his estimate of the distance far too small. When Columbus returned in 1493, John claimed his discoveries under the Treaty of Alcáçovas, and the dispute led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> expelled its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1492, John admitted tens of thousands of them at eight cruzados a head, for no more than eight months. About 20,000 families entered, and only 600 of the wealthiest won permanent residence. Those who could not leave in time were enslaved until Manuel's reign.</p><p>His only legitimate son, Afonso, died in a horse accident in July 1491. John named Queen Eleanor's brother Manuel as his heir in his will in September 1495, and he died of dropsy at Alvor on 25 October 1495. His remains were moved to the Monastery of Batalha in 1499. His epithet is taken to refer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>'s The Prince.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John II of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its dealings with Portuguese traders in the fifteenth century came at a turning point in West African commerce and contributed to the gradual shift from trans-Saharan to Atlantic-oriented trade; in 1488 the exiled Jolof prince Bemoim was received and baptised at the court of <b>João II</b> in Lisbon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>João II</b>, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488 and showed that an oceanic route to Asia was possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese priests baptised the ManiKongo, who took the name João in honour of Portugal's King <b>João II</b>, and his principal nobles were baptised too.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-3">3 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1482, on the orders of <b>King John II</b>, Diogo de Azambuja built the fortress of Sao Jorge da Mina at Elmina, after negotiating with a local chief remembered as Caramansa.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-gold-coast">Portuguese Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castle of Saint George, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Alvor, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.13&amp;year=1481" aria-label="Castle of Saint George on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 774 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John II of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1044h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.13&amp;year=1481">What was here in 1481? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
